<section class="py-5 bg-light">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="text-center mb-5">
            <h2 class="fw-bold">Frequently Asked Questions About Proforma Invoices</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="accordion" id="faqAccordion">
            <div class="accordion-item">
                <h2 class="accordion-header" id="faq1">
                    <button class="accordion-button" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapse1">
                        What is a proforma invoice?
                    </button>
                </h2>
                <div id="collapse1" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                    <div class="accordion-body">
                        A proforma invoice is a preliminary bill of sale sent to a buyer before goods or services are delivered. It details the items, quantities, prices, and terms but is not a demand for payment or a final tax invoice. It's often used for quotes, estimates, or customs purposes in international trade.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="accordion-item">
                <h2 class="accordion-header" id="faq2">
                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapse2">
                        Is a proforma invoice legally binding?
                    </button>
                </h2>
                <div id="collapse2" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                    <div class="accordion-body">
                        Generally, a proforma invoice is not a legally binding demand for payment. It's an estimate or quotation. However, it can form the basis of a contract if both parties agree to its terms. For a binding transaction, you would issue a final commercial invoice after the sale is confirmed.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="accordion-item">
                <h2 class="accordion-header" id="faq3">
                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapse3">
                        Can I generate proforma invoices for free?
                    </button>
                </h2>
                <div id="collapse3" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                    <div class="accordion-body">
                        Yes! RatPDF's proforma invoice generator is completely free. No signup, no hidden fees – just create and download as many proforma invoices as you need.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="accordion-item">
                <h2 class="accordion-header" id="faq4">
                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapse4">
                        What's the difference between a proforma invoice and a commercial invoice?
                    </button>
                </h2>
                <div id="collapse4" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                    <div class="accordion-body">
                        A proforma invoice is sent before a transaction as a quote or estimate. A commercial invoice is issued after the sale as a final bill and is used for customs, tax, and payment purposes. A proforma invoice becomes a commercial invoice once the buyer agrees to the terms.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="accordion-item">
                <h2 class="accordion-header" id="faq5">
                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapse5">
                        Can I add tax (VAT/GST) to a proforma invoice?
                    </button>
                </h2>
                <div id="collapse5" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#faqAccordion">
                    <div class="accordion-body">
                        Yes. You can include tax rates and amounts on the proforma invoice to give the buyer a complete estimate. However, the final tax invoice may need to reflect actual taxes due at the time of sale.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

